    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 100)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(5))  # F0..F4\n    customers = list(range(8))  # C0..C7\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(52, 66), (96, 84), (7, 85), (15, 51), (32, 31)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(90, 82), (69, 94), (90, 48), (17, 41), (66, 64), (77, 63), (41, 32), (75, 61)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [140.0, 123.0, 97.0, 176.0, 128.0]\n    customer_demands = [31.0, 31.0, 16.0, 13.0, 16.0, 42.0, 11.0, 43.0]\n    fixed_costs = [365.0, 195.0, 408.0, 431.0, 421.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.42  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Warehouse location decision and customer fulfillment plan\n\nTeam,\n\nWe need to decide which warehouses to open and how to route shipments to customers for the upcoming planning horizon. Our aim is to keep total costs low while ensuring every customer is fully served and service levels remain reliable.\n\nThe candidate warehouse locations are provided as map coordinates in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (52, 66)\n  - F1: (96, 84)\n  - F2: (7, 85)\n  - F3: (15, 51)\n  - F4: (32, 31). Our customer destinations are listed as map coordinates in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (90, 82)\n  - C1: (69, 94)\n  - C2: (90, 48)\n  - C3: (17, 41)\n  - C4: (66, 64)\n  - C5: (77, 63)\n  - C6: (41, 32)\n  - C7: (75, 61). This will help us understand which sites are closer to which customers and the likely lanes we would use.\n\nOn the cost side, opening any warehouse carries a fixed expense, as detailed in [365.0, 195.0, 408.0, 431.0, 421.0]. For distribution, we pay a transportation charge of 0.42 per kilometer per unit shipped. In practical terms, the farther we move product and the more we send, the more it costs.\n\nOperationally, each warehouse has a finite throughput limit, as specified in [140.0, 123.0, 97.0, 176.0, 128.0]. Every customer has a required volume that must be fully delivered within the period, as shown in [31.0, 31.0, 16.0, 13.0, 16.0, 42.0, 11.0, 43.0]. A warehouse cannot ship product unless it is opened.\n\nThe decision, then, is to select which warehouses to activate and how to allocate customer deliveries across the open sites so that we meet all demand without exceeding capacity, while minimizing the sum of opening and shipping costs. Opening more sites can reduce transport distances but increases fixed expenses; opening fewer sites saves on fixed costs but can lead to longer, more expensive hauls. We want the combination that yields the lowest total cost and is practical to operate.\n\nOnce we align on the trade-offs, we will recommend the set of warehouses to open and the assignment of customer volumes to those sites, along with the expected total cost and a clear rationale for the choice.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Memo\nSubject: Choosing which warehouses to open and how to ship to customers at the lowest total cost\n\nWe\u2019re at a decision point for our warehouse network. We have five candidate sites and eight customer locations, and we need to decide which warehouses to open and how to route product so that we meet every order while spending as little as possible.\n\nWhat we have on hand:\n- Candidate warehouse map coordinates: {FACILITY_COORDINATES}\n- Customer map coordinates: {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}\n- Warehouse capacities (how much each site can handle): {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}\n- Customer demand by location: {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}\n- Fixed opening cost for each warehouse: {FIXED_COSTS}\n- Transportation rate (per unit per kilometer): {TRANSPORT_RATE}\n\nBusiness ground rules:\n- A warehouse only ships if we choose to open it.\n- Each customer\u2019s demand must be met in full; no shortfalls.\n- No warehouse can be assigned more volume than its capacity.\n- Freight spend is driven by how far goods travel and how much we ship; shorter hauls reduce shipping cost, all else equal.\n\nWhat this means in practice:\n- Opening more warehouses can cut average delivery distances and lower freight, but each site carries a fixed cost to open and operate.\n- Opening fewer warehouses saves on fixed costs but likely increases average shipping distances and freight spend.\n- Some customers may be most economical to serve from more than one open site if that shortens routes and helps keep within capacity.\n- The right answer balances these trade-offs so we meet all demand without overloading any site and at the lowest total cost.\n\nDecision we need:\n- Select which candidate warehouses to open.\n- Determine how much each open warehouse should ship to each customer so that all demand is covered and capacities aren\u2019t exceeded.\n- Produce a clear cost picture that combines the fixed costs of the chosen sites with the resulting freight spend.\n\nExpected outcome:\nA recommended network design that names the warehouses to open, shows the shipment plan from each open site to each customer, and reports the total cost (opening plus shipping) along with the rationale behind the choice. The guiding goal is straightforward: minimize total cost while reliably serving every customer."
}